From 4b88c75f97c6f8d65532b06d2ea957348bb92d1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tylnexttime <tylnexttime@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:38:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Marian P1: routing-id trace of every MoE layer step
 (MARIAN_MOE_TRACE_IDS)

The P0 trace (MARIAN_MOE_TRACE) has counts per step and the fill trace
(MARIAN_MOE_TRACE_FILLS) has the ids of the uploaded experts only, so an
offline replay of cache or RAM-tier policies could not see the hits. With
MARIAN_MOE_TRACE_IDS=<file>, every MoE layer step whose ids reach the host
now records all of its routed ids, hits included:

  S  slot-map lookup of the MoE cache (ubatches up to 32 tokens), written in
     copy() right after the ids are read, with the S line's t_us and layer
  L  large-batch selective copy, once per layer and ubatch, written in
     sched_copy_experts when the first copy of the layer reads the ids, with
     that L line's t_us and layer

Binary, little-endian, documented in src/llama-moe-trace-ids.h: a 32-byte
header (magic "MRNIDS1\n", header size, version 1, 2-byte ids, open time)
and per step a 16-byte record head (kind, k, layer, n_tokens, t_us) followed
by n_tokens*k u16 ids in the router's order. A single-token decode step of a
10-of-512 model is 36 bytes: 2,400 decode tokens x 48 layers is about
4.0 MiB.

Off by default: one cached pointer test per step. When on, nothing that is
computed changes: the record copies ids the host already read, no extra
synchronize, no extra device read. Records go to a memory buffer; a writer
thread moves it to the file every 100 ms (or at 1 MiB) with the OS file API
(not stdio, so the C runtime's exit cleanup never races the thread), and
~llama_context flushes it. The writer is never destroyed, so no thread is
joined during DLL unload; a killed server loses at most the last 100 ms.

tests/test-moe-trace-ids.cpp (CPU only): the format byte for byte, ids that
do not fit in u16, the strides of the large-batch path, the background
flush, the destructor flush, an unopenable path. record() measured at about
70 ns per single-token step with 10 ids on a cloud x86 CPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
